<Section collapsed="false" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title>
<Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">9.2 <Font italic="true">n</Font>-ary Relations and Their Applications</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L100" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Using Maple, we can construct an <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-ary relation where <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is a positive integer.  As in the previous section, we will begin by defining types both for the elements of the relation (<Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">tuple</Font>) and for the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-ary relation (<Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">nrel</Font>).  The only difference here, as compared to the types we defined in the previous section, is that we do not know the length of the list that makes up a <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">tuple</Font>.  So we make a generic list with the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:type,structure" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Text" underline="true" foreground="[0,128,128]">structured type</Font></Hyperlink> syntax.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L101" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">`type/tuple` := list(anything);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUklbGlzdEclKnByb3RlY3RlZEc2I0kpYW55dGhpbmdHRiQ=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L102" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">`type/nrel` := set(tuple);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUkkc2V0RyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRzYjSSZ0dXBsZUc2Ig==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L104" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Consider the following 4-ary relation that represents student records.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L105" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">R3 := {[&quot;Adams&quot;, 9012345, &quot;Politics&quot;, 2.98],
       [&quot;Woo&quot;, 9100055, &quot;Film Studies&quot;, 4.99],
       [&quot;Warshall&quot;, 9354321, &quot;Mathematics&quot;, 3.66]};</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">PCU3JlEmQWRhbXM2IiIoWEIsKlEpUG9saXRpY3NGJSQiJClIISIjNyZRJFdvb0YlIihiKzUqUS1GaWxtflN0dWRpZXNGJSQiJCpcRio3JlEpV2Fyc2hhbGxGJSIoQFZOKlEsTWF0aGVtYXRpY3NGJSQiJG0kRio=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L107" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The first field represents the name of the student, the second field is the student ID number, the third field is the students' home department, and the last field stores the student's grade point average.  Note that this relation is of type <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">nrel</Font>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L108" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">type(R3,nrel);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L110" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">While we created a very generic <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-ary relation type, you can also create more specific types for certain particular situations.  For instance, the tuples in the relation above will always consist of a string, integer, string, and a floating point number.  So we could make the following type specifically for that kind of relation.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L111" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">`type/studentrecord` := [string, integer, string, float];</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">NyZJJ3N0cmluZ0clKnByb3RlY3RlZEdJKGludGVnZXJHRiRGI0kmZmxvYXRHRiQ=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L112" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">`type/studentrel` := set(studentrecord);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUkkc2V0RyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRzYjSS5zdHVkZW50cmVjb3JkRzYi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L113" drawlabel="true">
