<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L407" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">From this example, we can see that Warshall's algorithm can be a substantial improvement over the alternative, at least on this specific example.  The reader is encouraged to explore this further. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block>
</Section>
<Section collapsed="false" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title>
<Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">9.5 Equivalence Relations</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L425" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In this section we will examine how we can use Maple to compute with equivalence relations.  There are three specific problems that we will address here: given an equivalence relation on a set, how to compute the equivalence class of an element; how to determine the number of equivalence relations on a finite set; and how to compute the smallest equivalence relation that contains a given relation on some finite set.  Note that in this section, relations are assumed to be represented as in the start of this chapter, as objects of type <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">rel</Font>.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L428" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">First, we provide a test that determines whether or not a relation is an equivalence relation.  Using the work that we've already done and recalling that an equivalence relation is simply a relation that is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, this task is a simple one. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L429" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">IsEquivalenceRelation := proc(R::rel);
  if IsReflexive(R) and IsSymmetric(R) 
                    and IsTransitive(R) then
    return true;
  else
    return false;
  end if;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L435" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">As an example, let's define the equivalence relation &quot;congruent mod 4&quot; on the integers from 0 to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L436" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">makeMod4Rel := proc(n::posint)
  local R, i, j;
  R := {};
  for i from 0 to n do
    for j from 0 to n do
      if modp(i-j,4) = 0 then
        R := R union {[i,j]};
      end if;
    end do;
  end do;
  return R;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L437" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">makeMod4Rel(8);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PDc3JCIiIUYkNyRGJCIiJTckRiQiIik3JCIiIkYqNyRGKiIiJjckIiIjRi43JEYuIiInNyQiIiRGMjckRjIiIig3JEYmRiQ3JEYmRiY3JEYmRig3JEYsRio3JEYsRiw3JEYwRi43JEYwRjA3JEY0RjI3JEY0RjQ3JEYoRiQ3JEYoRiY3JEYoRig=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L438" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">IsEquivalenceRelation(<Label label="L437" view="label" executable="true"></Label>);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1615" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Equivalence Classes</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L440" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Recall that, given an equivalence relation <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and a member <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> of the domain of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, the equivalence class of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is the set of all members <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> of the domain for which the pair <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> belongs to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  In other words, it is the set of all elements in the domain that are <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-equivalent to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  So to determine the equivalence class of a particular element of the domain, the algorithm is fairly simple.  We just search through <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> looking for all pairs of the form <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, adding each such second element <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> to the class.  We do not have to search for pairs of the form <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> because equivalence relations are symmetric.  The following procedure returns the equivalence class for a given equivalence relation and a point in the domain.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L441" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">EquivalenceClass := proc(R::rel, a::anything)
  local u, S;
  S := {};
  for u in R do
    if u[1] = a then
      S := S union {u[2]};
    end if;
  end do;
  return S;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L443" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">As an example, we compute the equivalence class of 3 in the modulo 4 relation on the domain <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L444" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">EquivalenceClass(makeMod4Rel(30),3);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PCkiIiQiIigiIzYiIzoiIz4iI0IiI0Y=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1617" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Number of Equivalence Relations on a Set</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L446" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Next, we consider how to construct all of the equivalence relations on a given (finite) set.  The straightforward way to do this is to construct all relations on the given domain and then check them to see if they are equivalence relations.  Since a relation on a set <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiQUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiQUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is merely a subset of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, generating all relations is the same as generating all subsets of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1504" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To implement this, we begin by creating the set <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> using Maple's <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,cartprod" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">cartprod</Font></Hyperlink> function.  The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,cartprod" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">cartprod</Font></Hyperlink> function takes a list of sets and returns a table with two entries: <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">finished</Font> and <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">nextvalue</Font>.  The <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">finished</Font> entry is a boolean indicating whether the last element of the product has been reached.  The <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">nextvalue</Font> entry is a function which returns the next element of the Cartesian product.  This may seem a strange approach, but it allows for the conceptual creation of the Cartesian product of two sets without the need to actually store the entire product.  In this case, we do want to create and store the entire product, so we will use the following structure.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1505" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Cprod := combinat[cartprod]([{1,2,3},{8,9}]):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1506" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">CprodSet := {}: 
while not Cprod[finished] do
  CprodSet := CprodSet union {Cprod[nextvalue]()}:
end do:
CprodSet;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PCg3JCIiIiIiKTckRiQiIio3JCIiI0YlNyRGKUYnNyQiIiRGJTckRixGJw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L447" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To complete our equivalence relation procedure, we use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat,powerset" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">powerset</Font></Hyperlink> command from the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:combinat" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">combinat</Font></Hyperlink> package.  Recall that the powerset of a set is the set of all subsets.  So the powerset of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is the set of all relations on <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiQUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiQUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  Then we just check them one by one to see which are equivalence relations.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L448" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">AllEquivalenceRelations := proc(A::set)
  local C, AA, P, R, E, a, b;
  C := combinat[cartprod]([A,A]);
  AA := {};
  while not C[finished] do
    AA := AA union {C[nextvalue]()};
  end do;
  P := combinat[powerset](AA);
  E := {};
  for R in P do
    if IsEquivalenceRelation(R) then
      E := E union {R};
    end if;
  end do;
  return E;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L450" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">For example, there are 15 equivalence relations on <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L451" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">AllEquivalenceRelations({1,2,3});</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PDE8IjwjNyQiIiJGJjwjNyQiIiNGKTwjNyQiIiRGLDwkRiVGKDwkRiVGKzwkRihGKzwlRiVGKEYrPCZGJTckRiZGKTckRilGJkYoPCZGJTckRiZGLDckRixGJkYrPCZGKDckRilGLDckRixGKUYrPCdGJUYyRjNGKEYrPCdGJUY1RihGNkYrPCdGJUYoRjhGOUYrPCtGJUYyRjVGM0YoRjhGNkY5Ris=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L452" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">nops(<Label label="L451" view="label" executable="true"></Label>);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiM6</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1619" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Closure</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1742" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The last question to be considered in this section is the problem of finding the smallest equivalence relation containing a relation <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L454" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The key idea is that we need to find the smallest relation containing <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> that is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.  Recalling the previous section on closures, it is natural to think that we may compute the reflexive closure, the symmetric closure, and then the transitive closure, one after the other.  The only concern would be that one closure would no longer have one of the previous properties.  The following outlines why this is not the case.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L455" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" leftmargin="10" bullet="numeric" bulletsuffix="." initial="-1">First create the reflexive closure of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, call it <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L456" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" leftmargin="10" bullet="numeric" bulletsuffix="." initial="-1">Compute the symmetric closure of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and call this <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  Note that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is still reflexive since no pairs were removed from the relation and no elements were added to the domain.  So <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is both symmetric and reflexive.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L457" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" leftmargin="10" bullet="numeric" bulletsuffix="." initial="-1">Compute the transitive closure of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and name this <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  Note that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is still reflexive for the same reason as above.  And <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is still symmetric since, if <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> are in <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> to force the addition of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, then since <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is symmetric, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> must also being in <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> forcing <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> to also be included in <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  Hence, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is an equivalence relation.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L460" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We implement this method as the composition of the four methods <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">RelToMatrix</Font>, <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">ReflexiveClosure</Font>, <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">SymmetricClosure</Font>, and then <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">TransitiveClosure</Font>.  We use Maple's composition operator, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:atsign" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">@</Font></Hyperlink>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L461" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">EquivalenceClosure := TransitiveClosure @ SymmetricClosure @ ReflexiveClosure @ RelToMatrix;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUkiQEc2JCUqcHJvdGVjdGVkR0koX3N5c2xpYkc2IjYmSTJUcmFuc2l0aXZlQ2xvc3VyZUdGJ0kxU3ltbWV0cmljQ2xvc3VyZUdGJ0kxUmVmbGV4aXZlQ2xvc3VyZUdGJ0ksUmVsVG9NYXRyaXhHRic=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L463" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">As an example, recall the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">Div6</Font> relation representing the &quot;is a divisor of&quot; on <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  We can see that the smallest equivalence relation that contains <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">Div6</Font> is the relation in which every number is related to every other number.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L464" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">EquivalenceClosure(Div6);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L466" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">This is unsurprising, since 1 is a divisor of every number meaning that, in any equivalence relation containing the &quot;divides&quot; relation, 1 is related to every number.  We can make this example slightly more interesting by removing 1.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L467" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Div17minus1 := DividesRelation({$2..17});</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L468" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">interface(rtablesize=17):
EquivalenceClosure(Div17minus1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L470" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">(Note the first row and column still correspond to 1 because of the way the matrix is constructed in <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">RelToMatrix</Font>.)  In this example, you see that 11, 13, and 17 become isolated, being the three primes in the set which do not have multiples of them also included.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block>
</Section>
<Section collapsed="false" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title>
<Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">9.6 Partial Orderings</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L863" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In this section, we consider partial orderings (or partial orders) and related topics, including maximal and minimal elements, Hasse diagrams, and lattices.  We will explore these topics in Maple, and leave the exploration of other topics related to partial orderings to the reader.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1744" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Partial Orders and Examples</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1746" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">First, we will define a new Maple type for partial orders and create some examples of them.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1748" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Recall that a partial order is a binary relation on a set that satisfies the three conditions of being reflexive, antisymmetric, and transitive.  Previously in this section we defined various types (e.g., <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">rel</Font>, our relation type) via the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:type,structure" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Text" underline="true" foreground="[0,128,128]">structured type</Font></Hyperlink> syntax.  In this case, though, it is not enough to know the structure of the type's data, we also want to insist on certain properties.  We define the type to be a procedure that tests an object against the definition of a partial order.  It is very similar to the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">IsEquivalenceRelation</Font> procedure we created in the previous section.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L870" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Here is the definition of the partial order type.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L865" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">`type/po` := proc(obj)
   type(obj, rel) and IsReflexive(obj) 
                  and IsAntisymmetric(obj) 
                  and IsTransitive(obj);
   end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1508" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Unlike the structured type syntax, we explicitly define a procedure.  The main rules for defining types in this way are that the procedure must take one argument and it must return true or false.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L867" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Now we can use the name <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">po</Font> as the second argument to the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:type" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">type</Font></Hyperlink> command for checking to see if an object is of the type.  For example, we can check that the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">Div6</Font> relation we defined earlier (recall that this is the &quot;divides&quot; relation on the set 1 through 6) is a partial order.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L869" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">type(Div6,po);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L871" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">We create some additional examples of partial orderings that we can use in the remainder of the section.  The <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">Div17minus1</Font> relation (this was the &quot;divides&quot; relation on the set 2 through 17) is a partial order.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L868" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">type(Div17minus1,po);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L873" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Indeed, all the relations created via the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">DividesRelation</Font> or <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">DivRel</Font> procedures will be partial orders.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L881" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Next, we create a procedure to produce examples of a class of lattices (we will discuss lattices more below, for now it is enough that these examples are partial orders).  The <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">DivisorLattice</Font> procedure will create the partial order whose domain is the set of positive divisors of a given number and whose order is defined by the &quot;divides&quot; relation.  We only need to apply the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">DividesRelation</Font> procedure to the divisors of the given number.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L875" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">DivisorLattice := proc(n::posint)
  DividesRelation(numtheory[divisors](n));
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L876" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:numtheory,divisors" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">divisors</Font></Hyperlink> command in the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:numtheory" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">numtheory</Font></Hyperlink> package produces all of the positive divisors of the given integer.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L880" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">DivisorLattice(10);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PCs3JCIiIkYkNyRGJCIiIzckRiQiIiY3JEYkIiM1NyRGJkYmNyRGJkYqNyRGKEYoNyRGKEYqNyRGKkYq</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L882" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Finally, for a bit of variety, we create the posets whose Hasse diagrams are shown in Figure 8(a) and Figure 10 in Section 9.6.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
